From 7df9492e449e4b7e0bcffe8461e642f4ffab0b33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:46:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] vdn: encode the mp4 in parallel segments instead of one
 libx264 stream

Once the colour conversion moved to the GPU, the mux is libx264 and nothing else, and
x264's own frame threading is worth only 1.12x on a 864x480 canvas -- the serial part is
the per-frame Python plane writes, which hold the GIL. So the clip is split into four
contiguous ranges, encoded concurrently, and the bitstreams concatenated by copy.

Measured on 345 frames of 864x480, PSNR against the input planes:

    1 encoder,  thread_count=0     1.37 s   2.36 MB   42.72 dB
    4 segments, thread_count=8     0.43 s   2.42 MB   42.34 dB
    8 segments, thread_count=8     0.34 s   2.51 MB   41.63 dB
   16 segments, thread_count=8     0.33 s   2.65 MB   40.68 dB

Each segment costs one extra IDR, so this is not free -- 4 segments is +2.5 % bits for
3.2x. It is still a better trade than the alternative of a faster preset, which buys 1.9x
for 2.8 dB and fewer bits (a real rate-distortion loss, not a boundary cost).

Two things about the concatenation that a frame count and a PSNR both miss, hence
scripts/clipinfo.py. Every stream has to be declared before the first packet is muxed --
PyAV writes the container header on the first mux(), and an audio stream added after that
gets no time base, which surfaces as 'Cannot rebase to zero time'. And the re-stamping has
to be done in the output stream's own time base (mp4 picks 1/12288, so a 24 fps frame is
512 ticks): frame indices left as-is are read as 1/12288 ticks and give a 4952 fps clip,
while setting packet.time_base to 1/fps fixes the pts but rescales packet.duration a second
time and writes a 9.67 fps header. Both produce 345 frames in the right order.

Also corrects this file's own docstring: the claim that swscale cost 2.03 s of the mux came
from timing frame.reformat() per frame, which allocates a fresh frame each call. The
matched-config measurement is ~0.45 s for the conversion and ~0.40 s for thread_count.

